<DocsLayout
  title="Dual Rig & Dual Amp"
  description="Split Ardor into two independent chains for true stereo rigs, with dedicated realtime cores on a four-core Pi."
  lede="Two ways to run parallel rigs: a fixed Dual Amp block, or the fully flexible Dual Rig with two independent child chains."
>
  <SignalFlow />

  <h2>Dual Amp</h2>
  <p>
    Dual Amp is a single draggable block with two fixed parallel lanes. Both lanes receive
    the selected mono input; the left NAM → IR lane feeds the left output and the right
    NAM → IR lane feeds the right output. It exposes per-lane model, IR, nano selection,
    cabinet level, cabinet mix, and polarity.
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      An enabled Dual Amp block cannot be combined with standalone NAM or cabinet blocks in
      the same preset. Existing version-1 serial presets remain compatible.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Dual Rig</h2>
  <p>
    Dual Rig generalizes that fixed block to two <strong>independent child chains</strong>.
    It is a version-2 preset block and remains one draggable item in the outer chain. Each
    lane can hold any sequence of blocks — a different amp, cabinet, and effects per side.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Both lanes receive the same selected mono input.</li>
    <li>Each lane is processed in its declared order.</li>
    <li>The left lane's left output and the right lane's right output are retained at the fixed merge.</li>
    <li>Child block IDs are globally unique, both lanes must be non-empty, and split regions cannot be nested.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>In the editor a Dual Rig renders as an explicit <strong>SPLIT</strong>, separate color-coded <strong>LEFT</strong> and <strong>RIGHT</strong> rails, and a stereo <strong>JOIN</strong> — routing is never disguised as an ordinary effect card.</p>

  <h2>Parallel processing on hardware</h2>
  <p>
    Dual Amp and Dual Rig always have a sequential fallback: the audio callback processes
    the complete left lane, then the complete right lane. On a four-core Pi, Ardor enables
    an optimized parallel path:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Thread</th><th>Placement</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Audio callback</td><td class="mono">SCHED_FIFO/70 on CPU 2</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Right-lane worker</td><td class="mono">SCHED_FIFO/69 on CPU 3</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The callback processes the left lane concurrently and joins the worker once at the fixed
    merge. Effects before and after the parallel block still run serially on the callback
    thread. If the worker cannot obtain its realtime policy or affinity, the rig reports the
    failure and falls back to sequential processing — so audio never breaks.
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      The firmware image enables this layout by default via
      <code>PARALLEL_RIGS=1</code>, <code>AUDIO_CPU=2</code>, and
      <code>RIG_WORKER_CPU=3</code>. The UI and non-audio work stay as normal-priority
      Linux tasks that the realtime DSP threads preempt.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    See <a href={url('/docs/presets')}>Presets &amp; Storage</a> for the version-2 preset
    format that stores lanes.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
